DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The DTrace ssccttpp provider allows users to trace events in the sctp(4) pro‐
     tocol implementation.  This provider is similar to the dtrace_ip(4) and
     dtrace_udp(4) providers, but additionally contains probes corresponding
     to protocol events at a level higher than packet reception and transmis‐
     sion.

     The ssccttpp::ccwwnndd::::() probes track changes in the congestion window on a
     netp.  The ssccttpp::rrwwnndd::::() probes track changes in the receiver window for
     an assoc.  The ssccttpp::fflliigghhttssiizzee::nneett::vvaall() probe tracks changes in the
     flight size on a net or assoc and the ssccttpp::fflliigghhttssiizzee::aassssoocc::vvaall() probe
     provides the total flight version.

     The arguments of all ssccttpp probes except for ssccttpp::ccwwnndd::::rrtttt**() and
     ssccttpp::::aassssoocc::vvaall() are the Vtag for this end, the port number of the local
     side, the pointer to struct sctp_nets *changing, the old value of the
     cwnd, and the new value of the cwnd.

     The arguments of ssccttpp::::::vvaall() are similar to the above except the fourth
     argument is the up/down amount.

     The ssccttpp::ccwwnndd::::rrtttt**() probe arguments are a bitmap of Vtag << 32 |
     localport << 16 | remoteport, a bitmap of obw | nbw, a bitmap of bwrtt |
     newrtt, flight, and a bitmap of (cwnd << 32) | point << 16 | retval(0/1).

     The ssccttpp::ccwwnndd::::iinniitt() probe fires when a remotely-initiated active SCTP
     open succeeds.  At this point the new connection is in the ESTABLISHED
     state, and the probe arguments expose the headers associated with the fi‐
     nal ACK of the four-way handshake.

     The ssccttpp::::::sseenndd() and ssccttpp::::::rreecceeiivvee() probes fire when the host sends or
     receives an SCTP packet, respectively.  As with the dtrace_udp(4)
     provider, ssccttpp probes fire only for packets sent by or to the local host;
     forwarded packets are handled in the IP layer and are only visible to the
     dtrace_ip(4) provider.

     The ssccttpp::::::ssttaattee--cchhaannggee() probe fires upon local SCTP association state
     transitions.  Its first, third and fifth arguments are currently always
     NULL.  Its last argument describes the from-state in the transition, and
     the to-state can be obtained from args[3]->sctps_state.

